---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import Crt from '../../components/Crt.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';

const firstBoot = `Boot
: hp(0,0)unix
Unix Edition 8 -- ipnx Release 1.0 (2026-08-16)
real mem  = 8388608
avail mem = 7294976
hp0: RP07
network: il0 10.0.2.15, gateway 10.0.2.2

login: christie
Welcome to ipnx v8 (Eighth Edition Research Unix).

For copyright, please refer to /etc/copyright

Fortune favours the brave.
$ `;
---

<ManualLayout
  slug="getting-started"
  title="Getting started"
  description="What happens on first launch of ipnx: the account it creates for you, how to log in, and how the machine keeps its state between launches."
>
  <h2>First launch</h2>
  <p>
    Open the app and leave it alone for about half a minute. It is not loading — it is
    <em>booting</em>. The boot block reads the kernel off an emulated RP07 disk, the kernel probes
    its hardware, <code>/etc/rc</code> checks the filesystems and starts the network, and
    <code>init</code> puts a login prompt on each terminal line.
  </p>
  <p>
    While that happens the app also does something a 1985 machine could not do for itself: it
    creates an account. Your macOS short user name becomes a Unix account with a home directory
    under <code>/usr</code>, and the terminal marked <strong>tty01</strong> logs itself in as that
    account.
  </p>

  <Crt text={firstBoot} caption="A first boot, abridged. The fortune is different every time." />

  <h2>The account it makes</h2>
  <p>
    You are not root, deliberately. Research Unix has no <code>sudo</code>, so becoming the
    superuser is <code>su</code> at a shell — but ordinary work should not happen there, and the
    machine is more interesting as somebody's account than as an administrator's console.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is <strong>no password</strong>. This is a personal machine emulating a personal
    machine, on a disk you already have in your hands, and a password prompt with no recovery path
    would be a nuisance protecting nothing. If you want one, <code>passwd</code> works as it
    always did.
  </p>
  <p>
    The name is truncated where it has to be: Eighth Edition allows eight characters for a login
    and fourteen for any file name, and it does not negotiate.
  </p>

  <h2>Quitting, and coming back</h2>
  <p>
    Quit normally (<kbd>⌘Q</kbd>) and the app takes a snapshot of the running machine —
    processors, memory, open files, everything — and writes it beside the disk. Next launch
    restores it, so you come back in a couple of seconds to precisely where you were, mid-command
    if that is where you stopped.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Do not force-quit it.</strong> That is the one rule this machine has, and it is the
    same rule a real VAX had: a filesystem keeps data in memory that has not yet reached the disk,
    and pulling the power loses it. See <a href={href('/manual/troubleshooting')}>troubleshooting</a>
    for what a clean shutdown looks like and what to do if it happens anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>Where your things live</h2>
  <p>
    Everything the emulated machine knows is inside one file,
    <code>~/Library/Application&nbsp;Support/ipnx/v8/v8.disk</code>. You can copy it, back it up,
    or export it from Settings. Files you keep on your Mac are better kept on your Mac and
    <a href={href('/manual/sharing-files')}>reached through a shared folder</a>, which is what
    <code>/n/macos</code> and <code>/n/home</code> are for.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <p>
    If you know Unix, you already know what to do — but the two things worth trying immediately
    are <code>mux</code>, which downloads a window system into the terminal, and the manual:
    <code>man man</code> works, and the whole Eighth Edition manual is on the disk.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
